 "standard_text": "Multiplication of whole numbers to at least 10 × 10 (mental calculations); recall multiplication and division facts.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 4 times tables worksheets: multiplication and division facts to 10 × 10, strategies, multiplying by 10 and 100 and word problems. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "Know the facts to 10 × 10 by heart.",
 "key_sub": "One multiplication fact gives two division facts.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Know the times tables up to 10 × 10 by heart.</li><li>Use facts you know: 7 × 8 = 7 × 4 doubled = 56.</li><li>Every multiplication fact gives two division facts: 6 × 7 = 42, so 42 ÷ 6 = 7 and 42 ÷ 7 = 6.</li><li>To multiply by 10, the digits move one place to the left: 36 × 10 = 360.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>9 × 7</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>10 × 7 = 70</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>70 − 7</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 63</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Neighbouring facts</td><td>6 × 8 = 48, not 42.</td></tr><tr><td>Adding</td><td>6 × 8 is not 6 + 8.</td></tr><tr><td>× 0 and × 1</td><td>Any number × 0 = 0; × 1 keeps the number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does CAPS Grade 4 say about times tables?",
   "a": "Learners recall multiplication and division facts up to 10 × 10."
  },
  {
   "q": "How can learners practise?",
   "a": "A few minutes of mental maths every day."
  },
  {
   "q": "Which tables are hardest?",
   "a": "Usually 6, 7 and 8."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why learn division facts?",
   "a": "They are the multiplication facts in reverse."
  }
